  "subject": "🥊 Axios Finish Line: Fight better",
  "date": "Tue, 08 Sep 2026 21:29:19 -0400",
  "body_text": "96 \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n 🌫️Plus: Misty peaks | Tuesday, September 08, 2026 \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n View in browser \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n Axios Finish Line \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n By Mike Allen and Erica Pandey and Jim VandeHei &middot; Sep 08, 2026 \r\n \r\n \r\n Welcome back! Smart Brevity™ count: 412 words … 1½ mins. Copy edited by Amy Stern. \n\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n 1 big thing: How to fight more artfully \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n Book cover: Penguin Random House Most of us aren't taught how to fight well. We tend to bounce between avoiding conflict and \"burning the house down, \" author and conflict-resolution facilitator Priya Parker told Axios' Natalie Daher. \n\n Parker's new book — \" The Art of Fighting ,\" out today — is meant to help leaders, lovers, friends and family think more artfully about how they spar. Her last bestselling book, \"The Art of Gathering,\" was all about bringing people together. She believes her latest guide is in service of the same goal. Why it matters: Avoiding every disagreement can create what Parker calls \"unhealthy peace\" in a group, where the real conversations never happen and people are afraid they'll be punished for saying what they think.\n\n Conflict isn't necessarily a sign that a relationship or group is failing. Done well, she says, fighting can help people figure out \"who we are\" and even build trust. 📝 I asked Parker for advice on fighting better in a few familiar relationships:\n\n 💍 Marriage: \"Sit side by side\" when you fight, instead of facing each other. 🫶 Close friends: Start with \"I love you\" and \"I value our friendship.\" Parker says that signals the conflict is an investment in the relationship, not an exit. 📱 Family group chat: \"Let's take this offline.\" 💼 Co-workers/peers: Start with \"I love working with you, and I want to work with you for a long time.\" 👔 Boss-employee: \"Practice letting the arrow pierce\" — really listen, even if you feel defensive. Zoom in: Before confronting someone, Parker recommends asking yourself whether you can \"continue to engage meaningfully in this relationship without saying something.\"\n\n Also: \"Will I regret not trying?\" Yes, but: Sometimes \"strategic conflict avoidance\" can preserve a relationship — by changing what you talk about or even how you spend time together.\n\n ⚰️ Between the lines: Once in a while the healthiest move isn't to repair a group — it's to end it.\n\n Parker said a group may be ready for a \"funeral\" when it has completed what it set out to do, has \"lost its mojo\" and there's \"no longer a there there.\" Share this story . \n\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\nA MESSAGE FROM AXIOS\r\n\n \r\n Free Live Open House: See Smart Brevity in Action \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n Communication breaks down when it's unclear. Join our free live Open House to see how Smart Brevity sharpens every update your team sends.\n\n Live demo of the Smart Brevity method Real before/after comms makeovers Q&A with an Axios HQ coach 👉 Save your spot .\n\n\r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n 2. 🪞 Alaska, reflected \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n Photo: Bob Schumacher Thanks to reader Bob Schumacher of Wheaton, Ill., for another dispatch from his trip to Alaska!\n\n Bob captured the Johns Hopkins Glacier in Alaska's Glacier Bay National Park and Preserve. The tidewater glacier stretches roughly a mile across and rises 225–300 feet above the water at its terminus. \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n 🏁 Please invite your friends to join Finish Line . \n\n \r\n 👆 Like this comms style and format? It's called Smart Brevity®. Bring it to your org — via hands-on training or internal comms software — to harness its power and impact. \r\n &nbsp; Axios thanks our partners for supporting our journalism.\n Advertise with us. \n\n Read more about Axios' mission , ethics and how we use AI in our journalism . You can reach the authors by replying to this email.\nSponsorship has no influence on editorial content.\n\n Thank you for signing up for this Axios newsletter. To stop receiving this newsletter, unsubscribe or manage your email preferences . If someone forwarded this to you, sign up now to get Axios in your inbox.\n\n Axios, PO Box 101060, Arlington VA 22201 &nbsp; Follow Axios across:\n\n &nbsp; &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;",
