0\">&nbsp;</td>\r\n</tr><tr>\r\n<td style=\"padding:20px 3.5% 10px;line-height:1.5\">\r\n<span class=\"b k\" style=\"font-size:27px;font-family:'gorditamedium',Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;color:#222;letter-spacing:-0.1px;line-height:1.5\">  1 big thing: How to fight more artfully </span>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>   <tr>\r\n<td height=\"10\" style=\"height:10px;font-size:0\">&nbsp;</td>\r\n</tr> <tr>\r\n<td class=\"v\" style=\"padding:0 3.5%;line-height:1.75;font-size:17px;font-family:Georgia,serif;color:#222;letter-spacing:-0.1px\"><div align=\"center\"><!--[if mso]><img alt='' src='https://images.axios.com/b_HgrrSep_y3zdfgj1RaxUbshg0=/fit-in/1116x1300/2026/09/08/1788879022182.jpeg' style='display:block; margin: 0 auto; max-width:100%;' width='558' /><![endif]--><!--[if !mso]><!--><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://images.axios.com/b_HgrrSep_y3zdfgj1RaxUbshg0=/fit-in/1116x1300/2026/09/08/1788879022182.jpeg\" style=\"display:block; margin: 0 auto; max-width:100%;\" width=\"100%\"/><!--<![endif]--></div> <div class=\"bodytext\" style=\"font-size:13px; font-family:'Gordita',Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif; color:#848484; margin-bottom:20px;\">Book cover: Penguin Random House</div><p><strong>Most of us aren't taught</strong> how to fight well. We tend to bounce between avoiding conflict and \"burning the house down,<strong>\"</strong> <em>author and conflict-resolution facilitator Priya Parker told Axios' Natalie Daher.</em></p><ul><li><strong>Parker's new book</strong> — \"<a href=\"https://link.axios.com/click/47403713.377776/aHR0cHM6Ly93d3cucGVuZ3VpbnJhbmRvbWhvdXNlLmNvbS9ib29rcy82Nzg1ODEvdGhlLWFydC1vZi1maWdodGluZy1ieS1wcml5YS1wYXJrZXIvP3V0bV9zb3VyY2U9bmV3c2xldHRlciZ1dG1fbWVkaXVtPWVtYWlsJnV0bV9jYW1wYWlnbj1uZXdzbGV0dGVyX2F4aW9zZmluaXNobGluZSZzdHJlYW09dG9w/6a14a774a4e0095e0a03c94aB87bad364\" target=\"_blank\">The Art of Fighting</a>,\" out today — is meant to help leaders, lovers, friends and family think more artfully about how they spar.</li><li><strong>Her last bestselling book,</strong> \"The Art of Gathering,\" was all about bringing people together. She believes her latest guide is in service of the same goal.</li></ul><p><strong>Why it matters:</strong> <a href=\"https://link.axios.com/click/47403713.377776/aHR0cHM6Ly93d3cuYXhpb3MuY29tLzIwMjYvMDIvMDQvY29zdC1vZi1xdWlldC1jb25zdHJ1Y3RpdmUtY29uZmxpY3QtdGlwcz91dG1fc291cmNlPW5ld3NsZXR0ZXImdXRtX21lZGl1bT1lbWFpbCZ1dG1fY2FtcGFpZ249bmV3c2xldHRlcl9heGlvc2ZpbmlzaGxpbmUmc3RyZWFtPXRvcA/6a14a774a4e0095e0a03c94aB9e58ab98\" target=\"_blank\">Avoiding every disagreement</a> can create what Parker calls \"unhealthy peace\" in a group, where the real conversations never happen and people are afraid they'll be punished for saying what they think.</p><ul><li><strong>Conflict isn't necessarily</strong> a sign that a relationship or group is failing. Done well, she says, fighting can help people figure out \"who we are\" and even build trust.</li></ul><p><strong>📝 I asked Parker</strong> for advice on fighting better in a few familiar relationships:</p><ul><li><strong>💍 Marriage:</strong> \"Sit side by side\" when you fight, instead of facing each other.</li><li><strong>🫶 Close friends:</strong> Start with \"I love you\" and \"I value our friendship.\" Parker says that signals the conflict is an investment in the relationship, not an exit.</li><li><strong>📱 Family group chat:</strong> \"Let's take this offline.\"</li><li><strong>💼 Co-workers/peers:</strong> Start with \"I love working with you, and I want to work with you for a long time.\"</li><li><strong>👔 Boss-employee:</strong> \"Practice letting the arrow pierce\" — really listen, even if you feel defensive.</li></ul><p><strong>Zoom in: </strong>Before confronting someone, Parker recommends asking yourself whether you can \"continue to engage meaningfully in this relationship without saying something.\"</p><ul><li><strong>Also:</strong> \"Will I regret not trying?\"</li></ul><p><strong>Yes, but: </strong>Sometimes \"strategic conflict avoidance\" can preserve a relationship — by changing what you talk about or even how you spend time together.</p><p>⚰️ <strong>Between the lines: </strong>Once in a while the healthiest move isn't to repair a group — it's to end it.</p><ul><li><strong>Parker said a group</strong> may be ready for a \"funeral\" when it has completed what it set out to do, has \"lost its mojo\" and there's \"no longer a <em>there</em> there.\"</li></ul><p><em><a href=\"https://link.axios.com/click/47403713.377776/aHR0cHM6Ly93d3cuYXhpb3MuY29tLzIwMjYvMDkvMDkvaG93LXRvLWZpZ2h0LW1vcmUtYXJ0ZnVsbHktcHJpeWEtcGFya2VyP3V0bV9zb3VyY2U9bmV3c2xldHRlciZ1dG1fbWVkaXVtPWVtYWlsJnV0bV9jYW1wYWlnbj1uZXdzbGV0dGVyX2F4aW9zZmluaXNobGluZSZzdHJlYW09dG9w/6a14a774a4e0095e0a03c94aB35bfc74d\" target=\"_blank\">Share this story</a>.</em></p></td>\r\n</tr> <tr><td align=\"center\" style=\"padding:20px 3.5% 25px\">   <a href=\"https://link.axios.com/click/47403713.377776/aHR0cHM6Ly93d3cuZmFjZWJvb2suY29tL3NoYXJlci5waHA_dT1odHRwczovL3d3dy5heGlvcy5jb20vMjAyNi8wOS8wOS9ob3ctdG8tZmlnaHQtbW9yZS1hcnRmdWxseS1wcml5YS1wYXJrZXI_dXRtX3Rlcm0lM0RmYnNvY2lhbHNoYXJlJnV0bV9zb3VyY2U9bmV3c2xldHRlciZ1dG1fbWVkaXVtPWVtYWlsJnV0bV9jYW1wYWlnbj1uZXdzbGV0dGVyX2F4aW9zZmluaXNobGluZSZzdHJlYW09dG9w/6a14a774a4e0095e0a03c94aBd5ed9699\" style=\"display:inline-block\"><img width=\"32\" height=\"32\" style=\"border:0\" src=\"https://static.axios.com/img/axios-site/newsletters/logos/footer/share-facebook-circle-border.png\" alt=\"Share on Facebook\"></a> <a href=\"https://link.axios.com/click/47403713.377776/aHR0cHM6Ly90d2l0dGVyLmNvbS9pbnRlbnQvdHdlZXQ_\" style=\"display:inline-block;margin-left:7px\"><img width=\"32\" height=\"32\" style=\"border:0\" src=\"https://static.axios.com/img/axios-site/newsletters/logos/footer/share-x-circle-border.png\" alt=\"Tweet this Story\"></a> <a href=\"https://link.axios.com/click/47403713.\" style=\"display:inline-block;margin-left:7px\"><img width=\"32\" height=\"32\" style=\"border:0\" src=\"https://static.axios.com/img/axios-site/newsletters/log